{
  "id": "COMPOUND:27495 - PROTEIN:5940",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which (Z)-Pentadecenoic acid may interact with Carboxylesterase 2, through a series of target-mediated pathways involving Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP. This compound appears to act as a target for multiple compounds, including 2-Nonyl-4-hydroxyquinoline N-oxide and (2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[...], which in turn target Carboxylesterase 2. The presence of multiple distinct compounds that share a common target, Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP, indicates a possible regulatory or inhibitory role for (Z)-Pentadecenoic acid in this pathway. The evidence suggests that (Z)-Pentadecenoic acid may influence the activity of Carboxylesterase 2 through the modulation of its target enzyme, Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP. The integration of multiple evidence paths supports the hypothesis that (Z)-Pentadecenoic acid may exert its effects via a mechanism involving the regulation of this enzyme, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence does not provide direct evidence of a direct interaction between (Z)-Pentadecenoic acid and Carboxylesterase 2, but rather through its effect on the intermediate target, Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP.",
